{0 ocgtk}

The {b ocgtk} package provides OCaml bindings for GTK 4: the widget toolkit,
with windows, widgets, layout and the application model. They are generated
from GTK's GObject introspection data, apart from {!Ocgtk.Main} and
{!Ocgtk.Version}, which are hand-written.

{1 Modules}

The library is reached as {!Ocgtk}, with one module per class, interface or
record, such as {!Ocgtk.Window}, {!Ocgtk.Button} and {!Ocgtk.Application}.

{ul
{- {!Ocgtk.Main} — initialising GTK and running the main loop. }
{- {!Ocgtk.Version} — the GTK version, at run time and at compile time. }
{- {!Ocgtk.Enums} — enumerations and bitfields. }
{- {!Ocgtk.Constants} — constants. }
{- {!Ocgtk.Raw} — the C externals and abstract types the modules above are
   built on. }
}

{1 Dependencies}

GTK's supporting libraries are separate packages, each with its own index:

{ul
{- {!Ocglib} — GLib and GObject. }
{- {!Occairo} — 2D vector graphics: drawing contexts, paths, patterns and
   surfaces. }
{- {!Ocgdk} — low-level windowing, displays, input and events that GTK is
   built on. }
{- {!Ocgdkpixbuf} — image loading, scaling and pixel-buffer manipulation. }
{- {!Ocgio} — I/O, files, and the application and networking abstractions
   from GLib's application framework. }
{- {!Ocgraphene} — lightweight 3D transform and geometry types (vectors,
   matrices, rectangles) used by Gsk and Gtk. }
{- {!Ocgsk} — the GTK scene graph: render nodes and transforms for drawing
   widgets. }
{- {!Ocpango} — text layout and internationalized font rendering. }
{- {!Ocpangocairo} — glue between Pango text layout and Cairo rendering. }
}
